  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure has settled over the region and will continue to\ninfluence the are into early next week as it slowly drift east.\nTemperatures through the period will start in the low to mid \n60s today before gradually climbing into the 70s by Tuesday. \nOvernight lows will follow a similar trend with lows tonight \nfalling into the mid 40s to low 50s before becoming more mild by\nTuesday night in the mid to upper 50s. As this high drifts east\non Monday and winds become more southerly, the increase in WAA \ncouples with additional moisture nudging north will present the \npotential for isolated, light showers Monday and Tuesday but not\nexpecting anything widespread. As a result, the majority of the\narea will continue to remain dry through Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe dominant ridge that has influenced the area for the past\ncouple weeks will finally begin to breakdown as a deepening\nupper level trough pushes south across the central US. This\ntrough will progress eastward, ultimately moving an associated\nlow pressure system into the region on Wednesday and Thursday.\nThis system will likely bring widespread precipitation and end\nthe prolonged period of no rainfall throughout the month of\nOctober. There remains low confidence in the exact timing as it\ndiverges amongst models and model runs, but given the agreed\nupon robustness of the system, confidence is increasing that\nmuch of the area will see rainfall midweek. Temperatures through\nthe end of the week will remain above normal.",
